{"date": "May 29 - June 05, 2026", "brief_raw": "<SUMMARY>\nThe global AI industry is experiencing significant demand and investment, with TSMC reporting long lead times for AI hyperscaler capacity and companies like ZutaCore raising $100 million for AI data center cooling. Broadcom\u2019s AI chip forecast fell short, causing a market value decline of over 13% or $250 billion. Geopolitical tensions persist, particularly regarding US export controls on China's semiconductor industry, which China alleges disrupts global supply chains. Europe is advancing its tech sovereignty with Chips Act 2.0, incorporating photonics and curbing reliance on US cloud services. In West Asia, President Donald Trump signaled a ceasefire with Iran contingent on no American casualties, amidst ongoing concerns about the Iran war's impact on global oil supplies and regional energy security, including a reported Iranian attack on Kuwait. India is pursuing critical mineral partnerships with the UK and Venezuela and developing its semiconductor ecosystem with a Rs 27,000 crore Assam chip plant.\n</SUMMARY>\n<EWS>\nThe persistent high demand for AI chips is creating significant supply chain bottlenecks, as evidenced by TSMC's inability to meet hyperscaler demand and its CEO's statement that it will take \"a long time\" to match customer needs. This could lead to increased lead times and potential price volatility, despite TSMC's stated intention to keep prices stable. US export controls on China's semiconductor industry continue to be a source of tension, with China's Ministry of Commerce asserting these controls disrupt global supply chains, indicating potential for further retaliatory measures or sustained friction. The stalling of US-Iran peace talks and a reported Iranian attack on Kuwait suggest heightened military and geopolitical instability in West Asia, carrying substantial implications for global oil supplies and energy markets.\n</EWS>\n<EXEC>\n**Asia:**\nTSMC's CEO stated that it will be a long time before the company can meet customer demand for AI hyperscalers and indicated an intention to keep chip prices stable. AMD committed over $10 billion to Taiwan's AI supply chain. Taiwan optics leaders are pivoting from smartphone bottlenecks to the edge AI supply chain. Broadcom\u2019s AI chip guidance has raised questions regarding the APAC supply chain.\n\n**Europe:**\nThe EU unveiled a tech sovereignty package to boost chips and AI, aiming to reduce reliance on US tech and ensure no external entity has a \"kill switch\" over European digital infrastructure. This package includes the Chips Act 2.0.\n\n**Americas:**\nBroadcom\u2019s AI chip forecast fell short, leading to a market value decline of over 13%, wiping out $250 billion. Apple will use Google's cloud servers powered by Nvidia's Blackwell B200 chips for its reworked Siri set to launch in September, as Apple's own hardware proved too slow for advanced AI processing. AI funding has surged, with unicorn valuations up 70% since September 2024, and AI funding per unicorn increasing fivefold. The surge in electricity demand from AI and data centers is creating a \"renaissance\" for natural gas in North America, according to KKR & Co. partner Brandon Freiman. A proposed data center in Utah will be drastically scaled back following pressure from lawmakers. Microsoft showcased new tools for building and securely running AI agents at its Build conference. ZutaCore secured $100 million in Series C funding, with participation from Mitsubishi Electric, Carrier Ventures, Samsung Electronics, and Samsung Ventures, to scale waterless two-phase cooling for AI data centers. TSMC plans $165 billion U.S. fabs and is expanding U.S. land for AI chips. Infineon is exiting Mexico backend semiconductor operations and reviewing a site sale. Dutch chipmaker Nexperia, facing loss of China operations, plans to produce in the U.S.\n</EXEC>\n<LITHO>\n**Europe:**\nThe EU CHIPS Act 2.0 proposal has confirmed photonics as a new structural addition to EU policy, indicating a thematic bullish trend for the EU photonics sector.\n</LITHO>\n<REE>\n**Africa:**\nIn Agbogbloshie, Ghana, thousands of e-waste workers burn electronic waste to extract minerals, at a cost to their health. The EU is betting billions on South Africa as China tightens its grip on critical minerals. Brazil holds large lithium reserves but has barely begun to explore them, while the world focuses on lithium mining in Congo and Chile.\n\n**Americas:**\nA new $230 million battery manufacturing facility is proposed for Texas, aimed at scaling up the production of LFP cathodes for EVs and other applications. USA Rare Earth is utilizing CHIPS funding for activities beyond fabs. Canada's federal government launched an industry-led alliance to grow the skilled mining workforce, while Nova Scotia is launching a mining roundtable to identify development opportunities.\n\n**Oceania:**\nKalgoorlie Nickel has been granted lead agency status for its KNP-Goongarrie Hub, which is expected to streamline approvals and infrastructure pathways for the strategic project. The West is reportedly losing critical mineral assets to China.\n</REE>\n<GEO>\n**Asia:**\nThe US Commerce Department admitted failure to enforce AI export controls on China. China's Ministry of Commerce (MOFCOM) and Xinhua News stated that US export controls disrupt global semiconductor industrial and supply chains. Huawei credited bans for accelerating domestic chip independence. China also reiterated that US abuse of export controls disrupts global semiconductor supply chains in an OECD report.\n\n**Europe:**\nEurope unveiled a sweeping tech sovereignty plan to boost chips and AI, curbing reliance on US cloud services. A coalition urged completion of the Aughinish Alumina investigation, with the company insisting it complies with all applicable EU laws, including sanctions and trade regulations.\n\n**Americas:**\nThe House Committee on Energy and Commerce is urging the Trump administration to scrutinize China's role in fueling campaigns opposing data centers in the US, as a means of undermining the US in the artificial intelligence race.\n</GEO>\n<MILITARY>\n**Americas:**\nSD Government, a Gogo company, secured a multi-year blanket purchase agreement (BPA) with the United States Department of Commerce\u2019s National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) to support 'Hurricane Hunter' aircraft.\n</MILITARY>\n<CONCLUSION>\nThe technology sector, particularly AI and semiconductors, is characterized by rapid growth, intense competition, and escalating geopolitical considerations. The ongoing demand for AI infrastructure, coupled with supply chain constraints, underscores the critical role of foundries like TSMC and the strategic investments being made by major tech players. Export controls and national tech sovereignty initiatives continue to shape global industrial landscapes, with China condemning US restrictions and Europe seeking to reduce its dependence on US technology. The critical minerals sector remains pivotal, attracting significant investments and facing ethical challenges in regions like Ghana.\n</CONCLUSION>\n\n<INDIA>\nIndia and the UK unveiled new initiatives on critical minerals and maritime security, alongside a significant trade deal and a strategic defence framework. India's Supreme Court upheld higher mining royalties for state governments, ruling in favor of Karnataka against BMM Ispat. India is exploring a long-term energy partnership with Venezuela, with discussions between Prime Minister Narendra Modi and Acting-President Delcy Eloina Rodriguez Gomez covering crude oil supply diversification and cooperation in critical minerals. Union Home Minister Amit Shah highlighted peace initiatives in the Northeast, citing over 12 accords signed, and pitched the region as India\u2019s next IT hub, announcing a Rs 27,000 crore Assam chip plant. NVIDIA's AI PC push is expected to open opportunities for India\u2019s semiconductor ecosystem, building on Intel and AMD\u2019s AI chip capabilities. Indian equities ended largely unchanged due to West Asia tensions.\n</INDIA>\n<WEST_ASIA>\nPresident Donald Trump signaled a ceasefire agreement with Iran, conditioned on no American troops being killed. An Iranian attack on Kuwait was condemned by Rubio. The Iran war is highlighted as a factor in \"tank bottoms\" and \"very painful oil shortages\" globally, forcing countries to adapt to an \"Iran energy shock\" and exposing Europe\u2019s energy vulnerabilities. The Strait of Hormuz standoff has shifted the energy security debate. The Middle East conflict is impacting Asia-Pacific power markets and contributing to market volatility where US-Iran peace talks stall. The impact of the Iran war on ASX defence shares and ETFs was noted. AI in the Middle East is shifting from pilot projects to large-scale deployment. Companies are slated to add 40 million barrels of oil to the US Strategic Petroleum Reserve (SPR) after the Iran war ends. The Iran War warrants strategic shifts in US policy in the Middle East. Syunik\u2019s mineral wealth and strategic corridor in Armenia are turning the country into a strategic prize for the West.\n</WEST_ASIA>\n\n<FINAL_CONCLUSION>\nThe intersection of advanced technology, critical resource acquisition, and geopolitical stability defines the current global landscape. The relentless pace of AI development continues to drive unprecedented demand for semiconductor manufacturing capacity and related infrastructure, necessitating substantial investments and strategic partnerships. This growth is juxtaposed with persistent supply chain vulnerabilities and intensified efforts by major powers to achieve technological sovereignty through export controls and domestic industry initiatives. Concurrently, regional conflicts, particularly in West Asia, continue to exert significant pressure on global energy markets and reinforce the strategic importance of critical minerals. Moving forward, the strategic competition for technological leadership and resource control will likely intensify, requiring adaptive policy responses and diversified supply chain strategies to mitigate risks and capitalize on emerging opportunities.\n</FINAL_CONCLUSION>\n\n<KPI_METRICS>\n[\n  {\"Metric\": \"AI Chip Demand-Supply Gap (TSMC capacity)\", \"Value\": \"Long-term unmet demand\"},\n  {\"Metric\": \"Broadcom AI Chip Forecast Impact\", \"Value\": \"-13% / -$250B market value\"},\n  {\"Metric\": \"US-China Export Control Tension\", \"Value\": \"High (US admitted failure, China condemns disruptions)\"},\n  {\"Metric\": \"Iran War Geopolitical Instability\", \"Value\": \"High (Ceasefire signal, attack on Kuwait, oil shortages)\"},\n  {\"Metric\": \"EU Tech Sovereignty Drive\", \"Value\": \"Increasing (Chips Act 2.0, curbing US cloud reliance)\"},\n  {\"Metric\": \"Critical Mineral Supply Chain Vulnerability (West vs China)\", \"Value\": \"High (West losing assets, EU bets on SA)\"}\n]\n</KPI_METRICS>\n\n<FUNDING_DATA>\n[\n  {\"Entity\": \"ZutaCore\", \"Investment\": \"$100 million Series C funding\", \"Purpose\": \"Scale waterless two-phase cooling for AI data centers\"},\n  {\"Entity\": \"AMD\", \"Investment\": \"More than $10 billion\", \"Purpose\": \"Taiwan AI supply chain\"},\n  {\"Entity\": \"Texas (battery manufacturing facility)\", \"Investment\": \"$230 million\", \"Purpose\": \"Scale LFP cathode production\"},\n  {\"Entity\": \"Assam chip plant (India)\", \"Investment\": \"Rs 27,000 crore\", \"Purpose\": \"Develop semiconductor ecosystem\"}\n]\n</FUNDING_DATA>\n\n<MARKET_IMPACT>\n[\n  {\"Entity/Region\": \"Broadcom\", \"Impact\": \"Shares tumbled over 13%, wiping out $250 billion in market value due to AI chip forecast shortfalls\"},\n  {\"Entity/Region\": \"TSMC\", \"Impact\": \"Strategic price hikes could ripple through tech and crypto sectors due to AI growth, but CEO will keep prices stable due to long-term unmet demand\"},\n  {\"Entity/Regi