{"date": "May 29 - June 05, 2026", "brief_raw": "<SUMMARY>\nThis week saw escalating geopolitical tensions centered on the US-China technology rivalry and the ongoing conflict in Iran. The global semiconductor industry experienced a 14% year-over-year increase in equipment billings for Q1 2026, driven by AI investments, yet faced supply chain questions following Broadcom's guidance. The US initiated further regulatory steps for deep-sea critical minerals and faced political backlash over domestic AI data center expansion, with China accused of fueling protests. Europe introduced a technology sovereignty package to reduce foreign dependency. The situation with Iran intensified, with the IAEA reporting an increased nuclear risk, President Trump issuing warnings, and persistent rejection of ceasefires by Hezbollah, leading to wider economic impacts. India actively pursued critical mineral and energy partnerships, particularly with Venezuela and the UK, while also advancing domestic chip manufacturing and IT infrastructure development.\n</SUMMARY>\n<EWS>\nThe International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) concluded that the possibility of Iran developing a nuclear weapon is now higher than before the US and Israel attacked the country in February, signaling an increased proliferation risk. Broadcom\u2019s AI chip guidance has raised specific supply-chain questions for the APAC region, indicating potential vulnerabilities. China accused the US of abusing export controls, disrupting global semiconductor industrial and supply chains, escalating trade tensions. Lawmakers in the US accused China of bankrolling protests against AI data center construction, highlighting a new vector for geopolitical interference in critical infrastructure. The ongoing war with Iran is squeezing critical supplies and global alliances, impacting energy security, particularly concerning the Strait of Hormuz.\n</EWS>\n<EXEC>\n**Asia:**\nChina stated that the US abuse of export controls disrupts global semiconductor industrial and supply chains. China hit back at the US's semiconductor export controls. Huawei's executive credited bans for accelerating domestic chip independence.\n\n**Europe:**\nThe European Union unveiled a technology sovereignty package aimed at boosting local firms and reducing foreign dependency.\n\n**Americas:**\nSenator Elizabeth Warren invited Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang to a Senate hearing on China AI chip sales. GOP lawmakers accused China of bankrolling US data center protests and requested details from FBI Director Kash Patel and White House advisors.\n</EXEC>\n<LITHO>\n**Asia:**\nSEMI reported that global semiconductor equipment billings increased by 14% year-over-year in Q1 2026, reflecting continued AI-driven investment.\n\n**Americas:**\nTSMC plans $165 billion U.S. fabs. TSMC is tapping Nvidia accelerated computing to drive next-generation semiconductor fabs.\n\n**Europe:**\nDutch chipmaker Nexperia, facing the loss of its China operations, plans to produce in the U.S.\n</LITHO>\n<REE>\n**Americas:**\nNOAA advanced the Deep Sea Minerals Application in the latest U.S. seabed mining move, with Deep Sea Minerals Corp. receiving a \"substantial compliance\" determination for exploration efforts. Coeur Mining, Inc.\u2019s Chairman, President, and CEO, Mitchell J. Krebs, and Executive Vice President and CFO, Thomas S. Whelan, will participate in the RBC Capital Markets Global Mining & Materials Conference.\n\n**Africa:**\nBrazil sits on one of the largest lithium reserves but has barely begun exploration.\n\n**Oceania:**\nKalgoorlie Nickel won lead agency status for the KNP-Goongarrie Hub in Australia, which streamlines approvals and infrastructure pathways for the project.\n</REE>\n<GEO>\n**Asia:**\nMany US tech firms are turning to China's DeepSeek for cheaper costs and portability, citing that American models are too expensive. COMPUTEX 2026 saw Taiwan optics leaders pivot from smartphone bottlenecks to the edge AI supply chain. Gulf Development Pcl plans a $4.3 billion expansion for AI data center infrastructure in Thailand. Broadcom\u2019s AI chip guidance raised APAC supply-chain questions.\n\n**Europe:**\nThe European Union unveiled a technology sovereignty package to boost local firms. Europe unveiled a sweeping tech sovereignty plan to boost chips, cloud, and AI at home.\n\n**Americas:**\nSenator Elizabeth Warren is pressing Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang over export controls, China sales, Trump, and data-center policy. GOP Lawmakers accused China of bankrolling US data center protests. Kevin O\u2019Leary stated he would shrink his Utah AI data center project after political backlash. A California town voted to permanently ban data centers. Elon Musk's massive Terafab chip factory in Texas faces AI backlash. Canada wants to make its own AI to break free from US bots.\n</GEO>\n<MILITARY>\n**West Asia:**\nIran insisted ceasefire talks with the United States are proceeding and denied bombing the Kuwait airport. President Donald Trump stated the United States will win the conflict with Iran either \"militarily or on paper\" and suggested he would meet with Iran\u2019s supreme leader if it was to make a deal. The IAEA concluded that the possibility of Iran developing a nuclear weapon is now higher than it was before the US and Israel attacked the country in February. President Trump warned that killing American troops could trigger more pain, as Iran admitted to targeting US bases in the region. The Iran-backed Hezbollah militia rejected a new ceasefire in Lebanon, and Israel stated it would not withdraw troops from the country. The Iran war is squeezing critical supplies and global alliances. The Strait of Hormuz standoff has flipped the energy security debate. The US Energy Secretary stated that companies would add 40 million barrels of oil to the US Strategic Petroleum Reserve after the Iran war ends. Analyses suggest the Iran War warrants \u2018strategic shifts\u2019 in US policy in the Middle East.\n</MILITARY>\n<CONCLUSION>\nThe intersection of technology, critical resources, and geopolitical competition remains central to international dynamics. The US faces internal divisions over AI infrastructure development and external challenges from China regarding export controls. Europe is actively pursuing digital sovereignty to reduce reliance on external tech, while the escalating Iran conflict continues to pose significant military, energy, and humanitarian risks across the West Asia region and globally.\n</CONCLUSION>\n\n<INDIA>\nIndia is seeking a long-term energy partnership with Venezuela, with Prime Minister Narendra Modi and Venezuela's acting President Delcy Eloina Rodriguez Gomez discussing crude oil supply diversification and cooperation in critical minerals and mining. India and the UK unveiled new initiatives on critical minerals and maritime security. Union Home Minister Amit Shah chaired the 73rd Plenary Session of the North Eastern Council (NEC) in Shillong, noting systematic infrastructure development in the Northeast over the last 12 years, and highlighted a Rs 27,000 Cr Assam chip plant, pitching the region as India\u2019s next IT hub.\n</INDIA>\n<WEST_ASIA>\nIranian officials denied bombing the Kuwait airport and insisted ceasefire talks with the United States are proceeding. US President Donald Trump stated the United States will win the conflict with Iran either \"militarily or on paper\" and suggested he would meet with Iran\u2019s supreme leader \"if it was to make a deal.\" The International Atomic Energy Agency concluded that the possibility of Iran developing a nuclear weapon is now higher than it was before the United States and Israel first attacked the country in February. President Trump warned that killing American troops could trigger more pain, as Iran admitted to targeting US bases in the region. American Airlines is temporarily suspending some of its summer routes due to steep jet fuel costs amid the war with Iran. The Iran-backed Hezbollah militia rejected a new ceasefire in Lebanon on Thursday, and Israel stated it would not withdraw troops from the country. The Iran war is squeezing critical supplies and global alliances. The Strait of Hormuz standoff has flipped the energy security debate. The US Energy Secretary stated that companies would add 40 million barrels of oil to the US Strategic Petroleum Reserve after the Iran war ends. Analyses suggest the Iran War warrants \u2018strategic shifts\u2019 in US policy in the Middle East. A poll found President Trump's favorability dipped over inflation and the war in Iran.\n</WEST_ASIA>\n\n<FINAL_CONCLUSION>\nThe coming period is likely to be defined by continued technological competition, particularly in AI and semiconductor sectors, with both US-China rivalry and Europe's pursuit of sovereignty driving policy. The increasing domestic pushback against AI data centers in the US, potentially fueled by external actors, suggests rising internal friction regarding infrastructure development. The Iran conflict's escalation, marked by an increased nuclear risk and sustained military postures from all parties, signals a prolonged period of instability and economic pressure, especially on energy markets and critical supply chains. Strategic shifts in US policy regarding the Middle East are anticipated, alongside India's assertive pursuit of energy and mineral independence through diversified international partnerships.\n</FINAL_CONCLUSION>\n\n<KPI_METRICS>\n[\n  {\"Metric\": \"Iran Nuclear Risk Level\", \"Value\": \"Increased\"},\n  {\"Metric\": \"Global Semiconductor Equipment Billings Growth (Q1 2026 YoY)\", \"Value\": \"+14%\"},\n  {\"Metric\": \"US-China Export Control Tensions\", \"Value\": \"High\"},\n  {\"Metric\": \"US AI Data Center Protests Intensity\", \"Value\": \"Growing/Intensifying\"},\n  {\"Metric\": \"Strait of Hormuz Energy Security Status\", \"Value\": \"Flipped\"},\n  {\"Metric\": \"Hezbollah Ceasefire Acceptance (Lebanon)\", \"Value\": \"Rejected\"}\n]\n</KPI_METRICS>\n\n<FUNDING_DATA>\n[\n  {\"Entity\": \"ZutaCore\", \"Investment\": \"$100M Series C funding\", \"Source\": \"Mitsubishi Electric, Carrier Ventures, Samsung Electronics, Samsung Ventures\"},\n  {\"Entity\": \"TSMC (U.S. Fabs)\", \"Investment\": \"$165B planned\", \"Source\": \"TSMC\"},\n  {\"Entity\": \"Gulf Development Pcl (Thailand AI Data Center)\", \"Investment\": \"$4.3B expansion planned\", \"Source\": \"Gulf Development Pcl\"},\n  {\"Entity\": \"Assam chip plant (India)\", \"Investment\": \"Rs 27,000 Cr\", \"Source\": \"Indian Government\"}\n]\n</FUNDING_DATA>\n\n<MARKET_IMPACT>\n[\n  {\"Entity/Region\": \"Global Semiconductor Market\", \"Impact\": \"Record quarterly equipment billings reflecting continued AI-driven investment.\"},\n  {\"Entity/Region\": \"American AI Companies\", \"Impact\": \"Turning to China's DeepSeek due to high costs of homegrown AI models.\"},\n  {\"Entity/Region\": \"American Airlines\", \"Impact\": \"Temporarily suspending some summer routes due to steep jet fuel costs amid the war with Iran.\"},\n  {\"Entity/Region\": \"Asia Tech Stocks\", \"Impact\": \"Dropped after Broadcom rattled AI trade and dragged Wall Street names lower.\"}\n]\n</MARKET_IMPACT>\n\n<RISK_INDEX>\n[\n  {\"Vulnerability\": \"US semiconductor industrial supply chains\", \"Source\": \"US export controls (China's claim)\"},\n  {\"Vulnerability\": \"Global semiconductor industrial supply chains\", \"Source\": \"US export controls (China's claim)\"},\n  {\"Vulnerability\": \"APAC supply chain\", \"Source\": \"Broadcom\u2019s AI chip guidance\"},\n  {\"Vulnerability\": \"Critical supplies\", \"Source\": \"Iran war\"},\n  {\"Vulnerability\": \"AI computing demand\", \"Source\": \"Overwhelming everything, leading to bottlenecks\"},\n  {\"Vulnerability\": \"Smartphone bottlenecks\", \"Source\": \"Taiwan optics leaders pivoting from them\"}\n]\n</RISK_INDEX>\n\n<ACTION_MATRIX>\n[\n  {\"Date\": \"2026-06-04\", \"Actor\": \"US Senate (Se